# tabs.zd — one panel at a time, announced as a tab set.
#
# THIS IS AN ARIA TABLIST, AND NOW IT CAN SAY SO.
#
# The ARIA tabs pattern is `role="tablist"`, `role="tab"` with
# `aria-selected` on every one of them, `aria-controls` pointing at a
# `role="tabpanel"` with `aria-labelledby` pointing back, and a roving
# `tabindex` so the strip is one tab stop that the arrow keys move within.
# Six of those seven are written below. Until the `aria-*` arguments landed
# the vocabulary could spell exactly one of them — `role` — and this file
# deliberately wrote none, because a `role="tab"` with no `aria-selected`
# tells a reader that a control is a tab and never which one is chosen,
# which is worse than an honest row of buttons.
#
# WHAT IS STILL MISSING, and it is one thing: the roving `tabindex`. Every
# tab is its own tab stop rather than the strip being one, and the arrow
# keys do nothing. `tabindex` is not in the closed argument set and nothing
# in the language moves focus, so an arrow key could only move *selection*
# while focus stayed behind — the focused button and the open panel would
# disagree, and pressing Enter would jump the selection back. That is not a
# partial implementation of the pattern, it is a different and worse one.
# The panel is not focusable either, for the same reason.
#
# The strip is therefore operable and correctly announced, and it is one
# tab stop per tab. That is a documented deviation from the pattern rather
# than a silent one.

# The strip.
#
# `labels` is a `List of Text` and `selected` is the caller's `Whole` signal
# holding the position of the open panel. The pairing of a label with its
# position is `zip` over `indices`, because `each … in` binds the element
# and not where it came from, and a tab needs both: the label to show and
# the index to write.
#
# `group` is the caller's, exactly as `SearchField`'s `field` is, and for
# the same reason: `aria-controls` and `aria-labelledby` name elements by
# `id`, an `id` must be unique in a document, and nothing in the language
# gives a component instance an identity. A widget that generated its own
# would put two tabs on one page under one id and point both panels at
# whichever came first. The caller writes the word once and every id in the
# pair is derived from it.
#
# Three classes rather than one. `class` dresses the strip; `tabClass` and
# `currentClass` are what let a caller show *which* tab is open. That is
# class plumbing and not a look: this file still ships no colour.
component Tabs with labels, selected, group, class, tabClass, currentClass
    Row class is class, role is "tablist"
        each pair in (zip with left is (indices of (listLength of labels)), right is labels)
            Tab pair.second, index is pair.first, selected is selected, group is group, tabClass is tabClass, currentClass is currentClass

# One tab. Two `Button`s under an `if` rather than one with a computed
# class, following `examples/todo.zd`: the signal graph swaps the branches
# when `selected` changes and costs one anchor pair per tab that does not.
#
# `selected is yes` and `selected is no` are written out on the two
# branches, and the second of them is the half that matters. An unselected
# tab has to *say* it is unselected: a tablist in which no tab announces
# `false` is announced as one with nothing chosen at all.
#
# `controls` is written only on the open tab, and that is not an oversight.
# `TabPanel` renders nothing when its panel is closed, so an
# `aria-controls` on a closed tab would name an element that is not in the
# document — a dangling reference a reader is invited to follow to nothing.
component Tab with label, index, selected, group, tabClass, currentClass
    if index is selected
        Button label, class is currentClass, role is "tab", selected is yes, id is (group + "-tab-" + text of index), controls is (group + "-panel-" + text of index)
            on click
                set selected to index
    otherwise
        Button label, class is tabClass, role is "tab", selected is no, id is (group + "-tab-" + text of index)
            on click
                set selected to index

# The body of one tab, shown when it is the selected one.
#
# The panel is a sibling of the strip rather than something `Tabs` wraps,
# because a component places its `children` in one slot and a tab set has
# as many bodies as it has tabs. The caller writes one `TabPanel` per tab
# and gives each the index it belongs to, which is also the only way the
# two can be kept in step by the compiler rather than by a convention.
#
# `labelledBy` points back at the tab, which is what makes the panel
# announce the name of the tab that opened it rather than being an unnamed
# region. The tab is always in the document, so this reference never
# dangles the way the one on the tab would.
component TabPanel with index, selected, group, class, children
    if index is selected
        Column class is class, role is "tabpanel", id is (group + "-panel-" + text of index), labelledBy is (group + "-tab-" + text of index)
            children
